I found the CAP/DA policy document family, but the prefetched frame didn’t surface a transfer-specific section yet. Next I’m going to inspect the CAP/DA document structure, search for “transfer” language inside that policy, and check amendments so I can tell you the current governing rule.The search located the exact section: **3.3 Transfers of Eligible Beneficiaries** in CAP/DA policy 3K-2. I also confirmed this is the current CAP/DA document to inspect. Next I’m pulling the full transfer section, nearby context, and amendments so I can answer with the actual transfer rules and whether the beneficiary can switch providers.I now have the full transfer rule. CAP/DA policy 3K-2 expressly contemplates transfers and requires the case management entity to coordinate them to avoid service gaps. I’m going to pull the “free choice of provider” language too, because your question asks whether a beneficiary can transfer between service providers, not just between counties or agencies.## Short answer

**Yes.** The CAP/DA policy expressly allows transfers of eligible beneficiaries and requires the **Case Management Entity (CME)** to coordinate the transfer so there are **no gaps in services**. The governing rule is **Section 3.3, “Transfers of Eligible Beneficiaries,”** in NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy **3K-2, Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)**, effective **September 1, 2025**. I checked amendments for this document and the tool identified **Policy 3K-2 itself as the current authoritative document**, with no amending notice returned. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## What the transfer rule says

Under **Section 3.3**, the **CME must coordinate the transfer to prevent gaps in service provisions**. Before the transfer, the policy requires these steps:

- determine the anticipated start date of service;
- coordinate a transition plan between provider agencies;
- discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- initiate transfer of the electronic health record in the CAP Business System (e-CAP/DA) to the receiving county;
- arrange a home visit within **5 business days** after the beneficiary moves to the new primary residence, so the receiving entity can assess the home environment and identify health/welfare concerns and mitigation steps; and
- coordinate for services to **start on the first date of the transfer**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Rules for a CAP/DA beneficiary transferring to a different county

Section 3.3 gives a more specific rule for **CAP/DA beneficiaries transferring to a different county**:

- the transferring CME and receiving CME must coordinate the transfer;
- that coordination must occur on the agreed transfer date or **within less than 30 calendar days of the request to transfer**;
- both agencies must discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- the electronic health record must be transferred to the receiving county;
- the receiving agency must arrange the **home visit within 5 business days** after the move; and
- services must begin on the **first date of transfer into the receiving county**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## CAP/C to CAP/DA transfer rule

The same section also covers a beneficiary **aging out of CAP/C and moving to CAP/DA**:

- the CAP/C CME must start a transition/transfer plan **12 months before** the birth month;
- a transition plan is completed during the annual needs review at age 20;
- the beneficiary and caregiver must be consulted about other Medicaid and community resources after age 21;
- a multidisciplinary team meeting must occur **90 calendar days** before the birth month or transfer month;
- the local DSS must be notified in the month before the transfer so the CAP evidence code can be changed; and
- **CAP/DA services start on the first day of the birth month or identified transfer month**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Can the beneficiary switch providers?

Based on the text I retrieved, **yes, the policy contemplates changing provider agencies**, because Section 3.3 requires the CME to **“coordinate transition plan between provider agencies.”** That is the clearest retrieved language supporting a transfer between service providers. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

I also saw CAP/DA document headings for **“Free Choice of Provider”** and **“Freedom of Choice of Provider Form,”** but I was **not able to retrieve substantive text from those headings** in this run. So I can verify that the policy structure includes a free-choice concept, but I **cannot quote or rely on additional details** from that part without fuller text.

## Bottom line

A CAP/DA beneficiary **can transfer**, including in a way that involves a change in provider agency, and the governing rule is **Policy 3K-2, Section 3.3**. The core requirements are:

- CME coordination,
- no gap in services,
- transition planning between agencies,
- health/safety planning,
- transfer of records in e-CAP/DA,
- home visit within **5 business days** after the move, and
- services beginning on the **first date of transfer**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

