I found a federal-authority frame pointing to 42 CFR Part 441 as the main source, especially § 441.301 on waiver requests. The frame also surfaced 42 CFR Part 431 and 45 CFR Part 155, but 45 CFR Part 155 appears unrelated to a Medicaid HCBS waiver and likely noise. Next I’m going to pull the exact HCBS waiver sections and the CAP/DA policy itself, then check amendments on any NC document I cite.Search confirms the relevant federal waiver is the HCBS waiver under 42 CFR Part 441, Subpart G, with § 441.301, § 441.302, and § 441.303 being the core provisions. I also found NC’s CAP/DA sibling policy indirectly through CAP/C and Innovations search results, so next I’m pulling the exact federal sections and NC CAP/DA text that states the waiver authority. I’ll also check amendments on any NC policy document I use.I now have the exact federal sections and the current NC CAP/DA policy number, 3K-2, effective Sept. 1, 2025. The CAP/DA policy has a section that explicitly says Section 1915(c) of the Social Security Act authorizes the waiver and lists the required assurances. I still need to read that section directly and check for amendments to 3K-2 before citing it as current.## Federal authority for NC’s CAP/DA waiver

NC’s CAP/DA waiver is an **HCBS waiver under § 1915(c) of the Social Security Act**. NC’s own CAP/DA policy states that:

- **“Section 1915(c) of the Social Security Act authorizes the Secretary of Health and Human Services to waive certain specific Medicaid statutory requirements so that a state may offer HCBS to state-specified target groups of Medicaid beneficiaries who meet a nursing facility level of care…”** ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), effective 2025-09-01, current per amendment check).

The implementing federal regulations are in **42 CFR Part 441, Subpart G**. NC’s CAP/DA policy expressly cites that subpart as the waiver framework. ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment); [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441))

## What the state must demonstrate in its waiver application

Under **42 CFR 441.301**, a waiver request must include:

- the **assurances** required by **42 CFR 441.302**;
- the **supporting documentation** required by **42 CFR 441.303**;
- when applicable, requests to waive Medicaid requirements such as **statewideness**, **comparability**, or certain **income/resource rules**;
- whether the state will refuse HCBS if cost would exceed institutional cost;
- that services will be furnished under a **written person-centered service plan**;
- that beneficiaries are not inpatients and **would otherwise require the covered institutional level of care**;
- the **qualifications** of persons developing the plan of care;
- the **target group(s)** to be served; and
- clear descriptions of the **services** to be furnished. ([42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441), § 441.301)

### Required assurances
Under **42 CFR 441.302**, CMS will not grant the waiver unless the Medicaid agency gives satisfactory assurances, including at least:

- **Health and welfare** safeguards;
- **Financial accountability**;
- proper **evaluation and reevaluation** of beneficiaries;
- **level of care** and eligibility protections;
- **free choice** between institutional care and HCBS;
- adequate **provider standards/qualifications**;
- and compliance with **home and community-based setting** requirements. ([42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441), § 441.302)

NC’s CAP/DA policy summarizes its mandated assurances as:

- **Level of Care (LOC)**
- **Administrative Authority**
- **Qualified Providers**
- **Services Plan**
- **Health and Welfare**
- **Financial Accountability** ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), effective 2025-09-01, current)

### Supporting documentation the state must provide
Under **42 CFR 441.303**, the state must furnish CMS with sufficient information to support those assurances, including:

- a description of **health and welfare safeguards**;
- records and information supporting **financial accountability**;
- the state’s plan for **evaluation and reevaluation** of beneficiaries, including:
  - who performs them,
  - how they are done,
  - the evaluation form used,
  - procedures for written documentation,
  - procedures for periodic reevaluations;
- a description of how beneficiaries are informed of **feasible alternatives** and allowed to choose **institutional services or HCBS**;
- an explanation of post-eligibility treatment of **income and resources** where applicable; and
- documentation showing **cost neutrality**: average per-capita waiver costs must not exceed the average per-capita costs that would have been incurred without the waiver. The state also must identify the number of **unduplicated beneficiaries** it intends to serve each year. ([42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441), § 441.303)

## Bottom line

For NC’s CAP/DA waiver, the federal statutory authority is **Social Security Act § 1915(c)**, implemented through **42 CFR Part 441, Subpart G**. In its waiver application, the state must demonstrate to CMS that it satisfies the required **assurances** and provide **supporting documentation** showing, among other things:

- beneficiaries meet the required **institutional level of care**,
- services are delivered through a **person-centered plan**,
- beneficiaries have **free choice** of HCBS vs. institutional care,
- providers and safeguards protect **health and welfare**,
- the waiver has **financial accountability**, and
- the waiver is **cost-neutral** compared with institutional care.  

