The prefetched frame is partly off-target, but it did surface the HIV Case Management policy and a recertification checklist. I’m going to pull the actual HIV case management policy document, inspect the certification/decertification sections, and check amendments before answering.I found the right policy: Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, HIV Case Management. The search results confirm the exact sections on certification requirements and decertification. Next I’m reading those sections in full and checking whether 12B has any amendments/change notices affecting the current rule.## HIV case management provider certification requirements

Under NC Medicaid, a provider of HIV Case Management must meet the certification standards in **Clinical Coverage Policy 12B, § 6.2.1**. In summary, the provider must:

- have **a documented successful record of three years** providing or managing HIV Case Management programs;
- ensure services are provided by **qualified HIV case managers** meeting the qualifications in **§§ 6.1.1–6.1.3**;
- ensure case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors** meeting **§§ 6.1.4–6.1.5**;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV Case Management provider;
- comply with **applicable state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- maintain certification as a qualified HIV Case Management provider;
- comply with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- maintain a **quality improvement plan** for monitoring and evaluating case management records;
- allow **NC Medicaid or its DHHS-designated contractor** to review beneficiary, operational, and financial records;
- notify NC Medicaid/designated contractor of proposed ownership/name/address/phone/dissolution changes **within 30 calendar days of the proposed change and no later than five business days after the actual change**; and
- achieve **national accreditation within one year of Medicaid enrollment** through one of the listed accrediting bodies: ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC.  
  Source: [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.1 (effective 2020-01-15; amendment check returned no later change notice in the corpus).

### Staffing qualifications the provider must ensure

**HIV case managers** must meet one of the qualifications in **§ 6.1.1**, including specified degree/licensure and experience combinations. Examples include:
- master’s in a human services field;
- bachelor’s in social work;
- bachelor’s in a human services/related field with at least 15 semester hours in social work/counseling/public health plus 6 months of relevant experience;
- bachelor’s degree plus 1 year of relevant counseling/human services experience; or
- certain NC-licensed clinicians (RN, NP, physician, PA, or certified substance abuse counselor) with 2 years of human services experience.  
Source: [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.1.1.

**Supervisors** must also meet one of the degree/licensure and experience combinations in **§ 6.1.4**.  
Source: [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.1.4.

### Training requirements

The policy requires training for case managers and supervisors, including:
- basic HIV information and prevention,
- scope of work for case managers,
- core components of HIV case management,
- ethics,
- responsibilities/functions of the HIV CM system of care, and
- documentation requirements.  

It also requires **annual training** in confidentiality, cultural competency, current HIV disease management trends, ethics, core components refreshers, and treatment of people living with HIV; **6 of the 12 annual hours must be clinically oriented**.  
Source: [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.1.7.

### Initial/administrative materials

The policy’s certification materials also include administrative and program requirements such as:
- list of counties served,
- regular operating hours,
- after-hours response plan,
- community resource list,
- articles of incorporation unless a local government unit,
- organizational chart,
- ownership disclosures,
- access to required web-based applications,
- business plan and financial information,
- computer/HIPAA/electronic billing capability,
- precertification onsite visit,
- applicable licensure/certification compliance, and
- written policies on confidentiality, grievances, beneficiary rights, nondiscrimination, ethics, conflict of interest, electronic records, medical records, freedom of choice, transfer/discharge, and abuse/neglect/exploitation.  
Source: [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), administrative requirements section under provider certification materials (effective 2020-01-15).

## When can a provider be decertified?

NC Medicaid may decertify an HIV Case Management provider for any of the reasons listed in **Policy 12B, § 6.2.5**, including:

- failure to provide **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing**;
- owner conviction of a **felony charge**;
- failure within **30 calendar days** to develop, submit, and implement a written **plan of correction** for unmet program requirements cited by NC Medicaid or its designated contractor;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do not meet **§ 6.1** criteria;
- staff failure to complete mandatory **basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- staff failure to obtain required **continuing education units**;
- failure to provide staff supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation on time;
- failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing under **§ 7.5**;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely report agency changes, resulting in NC Medicaid/designated contractor being unable to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal/state laws, regulations, state reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **four months of certification**; and
- failure to achieve or maintain the certification requirements in **§ 6.2**.  
Source: [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.5 (effective 2020-01-15; amendment check returned no later change notice in the corpus).

## Bottom line

To be certified, an HIV Case Management provider must show organizational capacity, qualified staff and supervisors, required training, record-review and quality-improvement systems, site enrollment, compliance with Medicaid laws, and national accreditation within one year. A provider can be decertified for noncompliance with those requirements, especially fraud, records/confidentiality violations, unqualified staff, missed training/CEUs, failure to correct deficiencies, inadequate documentation, or failure to maintain certification standards.

